// Baseline for the browser host under WebDriver (issue #905).
//
// The first recording of this scenario. Until now `phoenix-perf report`
// correctly said "no baseline; nothing to compare" on every CI run, because
// nobody had written one. Adopted from the perf-browser-capture the smoke job
// uploaded on CI run 30688920069 — the green main build of e87c8711,
// ubuntu-latest — which is the same runner that compares against it.
//
// WHAT THIS MEASURES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Under WebDriver, `wasm_init` skips
// the render, audio, glTF and gizmo plugins, because a headless runner has no
// GPU for wgpu. `browser.frame` is therefore the ECS schedule and NOT
// rendering. The capture records its runtime as `wasm-automation` rather than
// `wasm-browser` precisely so it can never be silently compared against a real
// session, and this baseline inherits that: it is a budget for the simulation
// the page runs, not for the picture it draws. Measuring the real render path
// would mean the SwiftShader route the `*.capture.js` aids use, which measures
// a software rasteriser — a different thing again, and not obviously a better
// proxy for a player's GPU.
//
// THIS SCENARIO DOES NOT GATE. Wall-clock on a shared runner fails the gating
// rule in src/perf/mod.rs, and this one is worse than most: see the tolerances
// below. Reviewed again post-demo.
//
// TOLERANCES ARE WIDE ON PURPOSE, and wider than vellum-perf's 0.25 / 1.0
// defaults that the first recording gave them:
//
// - browser.frame is 0.8 ms at p95. A ratio against a sub-millisecond number
//   is nearly meaningless — the clock's own resolution shows up as 0.1 ms
//   steps, which is already a 12% "drift" — so the default warn of 0.25 would
//   fire on quantisation. Held at 1.0 / 4.0: the reading worth having is "a
//   frame now costs several times what it did", not "a frame moved by a tick
//   of the clock".
// - browser.boot is a SINGLE sample. One observation has no spread to
//   calibrate against, so its tolerance is a guess and is set loose enough to
//   admit one; tighten it when a run of green captures says what the spread
//   actually is.
//
// Commentary belongs in this header: `phoenix-perf adopt` regenerates the RON
// value below from a capture, so a comment written inside it would be deleted
// by the next recording. See src/perf/baseline.rs.
//
// ── Recorded from a capture ─────────────────────────────────
// Written by `phoenix-perf adopt` from a wasm-automation capture of scenario "browser-automation".
// build: release   device: (unrecorded)
// rev:   (unrecorded)
//
// Edit the prose ABOVE this block; re-recording rewrites from here down, and
// carries every statistic and tolerance over unchanged. The numbers are the
// capture's; the tolerances are yours.
(
    scenario: "browser-automation",
    expectations: {
        "browser.boot": (
            unit: millis,
            statistic: p95,
            expected: 83.29999999998836,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.5,
                fail: 2.0,
            ),
        ),
        "browser.frame": (
            unit: millis,
            statistic: p95,
            expected: 0.8000000000465661,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 1.0,
                fail: 4.0,
            ),
        ),
    },
)
